> Our assessment of agricultural impacts follows the comprehensive framework developed in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}, combining three distinct modelling approaches into a single probabilistic estimate of climate damages to the UK agricultural sector. First, we use spatially explicit projections of arable land from \citet{ritchie2019large}, based on the ECOnometric AGricultural land use model (ECO-AG), which estimates annual arable area at \SI{2}{km} resolution for 1998--2098 and aggregates these to a 10\arcsecond{} grid. For each grid cell, we fit a reduced-form damage function on the log of arable area as a quadratic function of local temperature change, using temperatures from the HadRM3-PPE-UK ensemble (ECS \SI{3.5}{\celsius}), with separate fits for scenarios with and without a weakening of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) (see \cref{fig:arable-dfs}). We then embed these two arable-land futures in a stochastic tipping-point framework that links global temperature to AMOC weakening. In a first calibration, we assign probabilistic weights to the with- and without-AMOC damage functions at the level of each FaIR Monte Carlo run, using constraints on expected AMOC weakening from \citet{ipccar6wg1ch9} (Figure~\ref{fig:ipcccal}). In a second calibration, we follow \citet{dietz2021economic} and \citet{anthoff2016shutting}, specifying an annual hazard of AMOC triggering that increases with global mean temperature and, once triggered, phases in the with-AMOC damage function over 30 years (\cref{fig:agmaps-dietz}). In both cases, local fractional losses in arable land are translated proportionally into losses in horticultural production by grid cell, without allowing for endogenous crop switching or alternative land uses.
> 
> Second, we incorporate direct agricultural productivity impacts from the COACCH project \citepalias{boered2019d22}. We construct NUTS2-level temperature series from an ensemble of CMIP-class GCMs (including HadGEM2-ES, EC-EARTH, IPSL-CM5A-LR, GFDL-ESM2M, MIROC-ESM-CHEM, NorESM1-M, and MPI-ESM-LR), and estimate regional quadratic damage functions relating historical plus RCP8.5 temperature anomalies to percentage changes in agricultural gross output (\cref{fig:agri-coacch-df}). Third, we use the multi-crop productivity projections from \citetalias{feyen2020climate}, which report yield impacts for five major grains covering roughly 70\% of UK cropped area, and map these into changes in sectoral output.\footnote{For details on the PESETA projection procedure and aggregation, see \cref{sec:pesetaapx}.}
> 
