> \subsubsection{Technical summary}
> 
> We estimate climate damages to UK agriculture by combining three independent modelling approaches into a single set of projections and treating their differences as model uncertainty. Each model is first translated into a monetary shock to agricultural output, then into economy-wide GDP impacts via general equilibrium multipliers.
> 
> The first approach uses spatially explicit projections of arable land from \citet{ritchie2019large}, based on the ECOnometric AGricultural land use model (ECO-AG). Annual arable area is projected at \SI{2}{km} resolution for 1998--2098 and aggregated to a 10\arcsecond{} grid. For each grid cell, we estimate a reduced-form damage function for arable land as a quadratic function of local temperature change relative to a 1998--2008 baseline:
> \[
> \log{\text{ArableArea}_t} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \Delta T_t + \beta_2 \Delta T_t^2,
> \]
> where $\Delta T_t$ is derived from the HadRM3-PPE-UK ensemble with a \SI{3.5}{\celsius} equilibrium climate sensitivity, consistent with the original study. We use separate damage functions for scenarios with and without a weakening of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), derived from experiments with and without freshwater hosing in HadGEM3. These two sets of grid-cell damage functions form the basis for our AMOC-tipping analysis.
> 
> We then embed these arable-land damage functions in two alternative probabilistic AMOC frameworks. In the first, we assign a probabilistic weight to the with-AMOC and without-AMOC scenarios for each FaIR Monte Carlo run, drawing the expected AMOC weakening and its uncertainty from constraints reported in \citet{ipccar6wg1ch9}. In the second, following \citet{dietz2021economic} and \citet{anthoff2016shutting}, we specify an annual hazard of AMOC triggering that increases with global mean temperature,
> \[
> p_{AMOC,t} = 1 - \exp(-b_{AMOC}\,\Delta T_t),
> \]
> with $b_{AMOC}$ calibrated to the least-likely Hadley scenario in \citet{anthoff2016shutting}. Once a Bernoulli trial indicates triggering, the with-AMOC damages are phased in linearly over 30 years. In both calibrations, local fractional reductions in arable land are assumed to translate proportionally into reductions in horticultural production in the same grid cell, without allowing for endogenous crop switching or alternative land uses. These physical output changes are then aggregated to UK-level losses in agricultural production value.
> 
> The second approach uses the COACCH agricultural damage estimates \citepalias{boered2019d22}. We construct NUTS2-level temperature series from an ensemble of CMIP-class GCMs (including HadGEM2-ES, EC-EARTH, IPSL-CM5A-LR, GFDL-ESM2M, MIROC-ESM-CHEM, NorESM1-M, and MPI-ESM-LR), derive historical and RCP8.5 temperature anomalies, and estimate regional quadratic damage functions linking these anomalies to percentage changes in agricultural gross output at NUTS2 level. These percentage shocks are then applied to regional agricultural value added and aggregated to the national level.
> 
> The third approach uses the PESETA results from \citetalias{feyen2020climate}, which provide multi-crop yield and productivity impacts for five major grains that together cover about 70\% of UK cropped land. We convert gridded or regional yield impacts into changes in national agricultural output, assuming proportionality between yield and production value for the covered crop group. Other crops are assumed to be unaffected in this model, implying that the PESETA-based estimates may understate total agricultural damages.\footnote{For further details on the PESETA projection process and aggregation, see \cref{sec:pesetaapx}.}
> 
> For each of the three approaches, we obtain a time series of direct agricultural output losses in millions of GBP. These are translated into economy-wide GDP impacts using multiplier estimates from \citet{ciscar2019assessing}, which imply that total losses to the UK economy are 2.14 times the direct agricultural losses, reflecting supply-chain and general equilibrium effects. We then express results as percentage losses relative to a no-damage GDP baseline.
> 
> Finally, we combine the three agricultural impact models using a simple meta-analytic procedure that treats them as alternative structural specifications of the agricultural damage function. At each time point, we form a central estimate as a weighted average across models and interpret the spread across models as a measure of structural model uncertainty, which is reflected in the ranges reported in the main text. The full combination protocol and its application across sectors are documented in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}, which also provides additional implementation details not repeated here.
