> \subsubsection{Methods}
> 
> We rely on results from CIAM \citep{diaz2016estimating} and DIVA \citepalias{lincked2019d23}, both of which use sophisticated engineering-economic models of coastal risks and adaptation. Both models can calculate ``optimal'' coastal policies, where foreseen risks are minimized through adaptation actions. In the absence of evidence about the level of adaptation that will actually be observed, we treat this as a form of uncertainty.
> 
> \paragraph{Sea-level rise estimates}
> 
> The global mean sea-level rise (GSLR) estimates use the semi-empirical model from Kopp et al. \citep{kopp2016temperature}. To match the FaIR model results to the semi-empirical model, we randomly associate each Monte Carlo draw with a Monte Carlo run from HadCRUT describing historical temperature uncertainty. Kopp et al. provide two calibrations (based on different datasets), each with uncertainty, so we also draw randomly from both sets of uncertain parameter values.
> 
> To prepare these uncertainty sea-level rise values for running in Coastal Impact and Adaptation Model (CIAM), our source for damages, we generate 1000 bootstraps of these runs, each time finding the run closest to particular desired quantile in 2100. The desired quantiles are the 5\% quantile of GSLR, 50\% quantile, and 95\% quantile. These are shown in \cref{fig:gslr}.
> 
> \begin{figure}
>     \centering
>     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/channels/slr/gslr.pdf}
>     \caption{Global sea level rise quantiles, according to the FaIR temperatures and the semi-empirical model from \citet{kopp2016temperature}.}
>     \label{fig:gslr}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \paragraph{Impact estimation}
> 
> CIAM has been built to study the impacts of sea level rise on coastlines worldwide, under different assumptions about adaptation that include the possibility of optimal (least-cost) adaptation planning. The model was introduced and is described in detail in \citet{diaz2016estimating}.
> 
> CIAM works at a high spatial resolution by building on the Dynamic Interactive Vulnerability Assessment (DIVA) database \citep{hinkel2009integrating}. DIVA partitions the world’s coastlines into \num{12148} segments with homogeneous physical characteristics. The median length of a segment is 18km. The UK’s coastline is divided into \num{402} segments. Cost estimates at the segment level can be aggregated to coarser spatial scales, for example the country and global levels. CIAM runs in time steps of 10 years. It is initialised in the year 2000 and can run up to 2200, though we mainly focus on the period up to 2100. The base year currency is 2010 US dollars. We convert this to 2020 US dollars using a decadal inflation rate of 20\%.
> 
> Each segment in CIAM is described by its physical attributes (coastline length, surface area by elevation, storm surge frequency, wetland extent), socio-economic attributes (income, population density, capital stock), as well as protection costs. Physical attributes are taken from DIVA, while socio-economic attributes and protection costs are derived from a range of sources described in \citet{diaz2016estimating}. The resulting database includes estimates of land value and the stock of immobile capital such as infrastructure and buildings.
> While some segment attributes such as length are time-invariant, most are time-varying. Future segment population is based on the United Nations’ projections, while future segment income is based on IMF projections.
> 
> There are five categories of coastal impact in CIAM:
> \begin{itemize}
> \item Protection costs (construction of sea walls, dikes, etc.);
> \item Retreat costs (relocating people and mobile capital inland, and demolishing immobile capital);
> \item Inundation costs (the value of lost land, and immobile capital abandoned);
> \item Wetland costs (the value of lost wetland ecosystem services);
> \item Flood costs (expected damage to capital stocks and expected mortality from storm surges).
> \end{itemize}
> 
> The overall cost of sea level rise is the sum of each of these five cost categories, the first two of which can be regarded as adaptation costs, while the latter three can be regarded as residual damages.
> 
> Both overall costs and their composition depends on what is assumed about adaptation. In general, each segment possesses a social planner who solves the following optimisation problem for his/her own segment:
> \begin{align*}
> \text{OverallCost} =& \min_s \sum_{t \in \Delta t} \left[\frac{1}{(1+r)^t}\left(\text{ProtectionCost}_{st} + \text{RetreatCost}_{st} + \right.\right.\\
> & \left.\left.\text{InundationCost}_{st} +\text{WetlandCost}_{st} + \text{FloodCost}_{st} \right)\right]
> \end{align*}
> where $s$ is the adaptation strategy, $\Delta t$ is the adaptation planning period comprising decadal time-steps $t$, and $r$ is the discount rate.
> 
> When the adaptation strategy $s$ corresponds to no adaptation, $\text{ProtectionCost}_t = 0$ for all $t$ and $\text{RetreatCost}_t$ is optimally chosen to react to sea level rise at the same time step. Alternatively, when $s$ corresponds to optimal adaptation, both $\text{ProtectionCost}$ and $\text{RetreatCost}$ are chosen once during each adaptation planning period in order to minimise the discounted $\text{OverallCost}$ of sea level rise in the current and next adaptation planning periods.
> 
> This problem is solved independently for each of CIAM’s \num{12148} segments and sequentially for each adaptation planning period. Thus, when adapting optimally the planner looks forward one period in each planning period. Perfect foresight is assumed, which is unrealistic but necessary for computational tractability given so many optimisation problems are being solved simultaneously. The default adaptation planning periods are 40 years, with shorter initial and final periods, i.e.: 2000; 2010-2040; 2050-2090; 2100-2140; 2150-2180; 2190-2200.
> 
> The resulting damages, in terms of billions of GDP lost, are in \cref{tbl:slr-billion}. These are then converted to the \% GDP shown in the main text.
