> \subsubsection{Technical summary}
> 
> We quantify mortality-related health impacts from climate change using two complementary modelling approaches that both focus on temperature-related deaths, and then monetise these impacts using standard UK values of a prevented fatality (VPF). The first approach uses the PESETA IV estimates of deaths from heatwaves and cold spells \citepalias{feyen2020climate}, based on the empirical relationships assembled in \citet{naumann2020global}. The second uses country-level mortality projections from \citet{bressler2021estimates}, who extend one of the most comprehensive epidemiological studies of temperature–mortality relationships to project future deaths under alternative warming scenarios. We treat these as structurally distinct models of temperature-related mortality for the UK, and combine them via meta-analysis. Non-fatal health outcomes are not explicitly included.
> 
> For the PESETA IV component, \citetalias{feyen2020climate} estimate changes in mortality from heat and cold events using observed relationships between temperature extremes and attributed deaths in each country. Their approach distinguishes heat-related and cold-related excess mortality, estimates exposure–response curves based on historical data, and applies these to climate projections to obtain future changes in deaths from heatwaves and cold spells. We extract the projected changes in annual temperature-related death rates for the UK \& Ireland region and interpret these as representative for the UK. These projections are expressed as changes in deaths per 100{,}000 population as a function of global mean temperature (and time), conditional on a given socio-economic scenario. We then convert these changes in death rates into annual counts of premature deaths at the national level using projections of UK population, and ultimately into monetary losses using the UK Department for Transport’s VPF.
> 
> The Bressler et al.\ model \citep{bressler2021estimates} starts from a large multi-country epidemiological evidence base on daily temperature–mortality relationships. It fits nonlinear exposure–response functions that capture both heat and cold risks and then combines these with gridded climate projections and demographic projections to project changes in mortality attributable to climate change at the country level. From the supplementary materials, we take the projected UK temperature-attributable deaths (interpreting them as the net balance of additional heat-related deaths and avoided cold-related deaths). To integrate these into our standard damage-function framework, we modify our usual fitting procedure: instead of fitting separate functions to heat and cold deaths, we treat total temperature-related mortality as the sum of two latent components (heat and cold). We fit a reduced-form damage function directly to the net mortality projections, while conceptually maintaining the decomposition into heat-related and cold-related components as latent variables. Uncertainty is represented by the bounds reported by \citet{bressler2021estimates}, which we interpret as defining a distribution around the latent net-mortality trajectory.
> 
> We do not implement the income-adaptation adjustments reported by \citet{bressler2021estimates}. Those adjustments reflect joint changes in income and climate and are not reported relative to a counterfactual with income growth but without climate change. Since our objective is to isolate the marginal effect of climate conditional on socio-economic trajectories used elsewhere in the assessment, we use only the climate-driven component of their mortality projections.
> 
> For both modelling approaches, we convert projected changes in deaths into monetary welfare losses using a value of a prevented fatality consistent with UK policy appraisal. Our baseline valuation uses a VPF of \pounds1.83\,million in 2016 prices (equivalent to approximately \pounds1.65\,million in 2010 prices) from the UK Transport Analysis Guidance data book \citep{dft2016transport}, following the UK Green Book. We note that alternative literature-based estimates suggest higher central values (on the order of \pounds9–12\,million per fatality) \citep{thomas2017value}, but we do not adopt these higher values in our main calculations. Future work could explore the sensitivity of aggregate health damages to alternative VPF assumptions.
> 
> The output of each model is thus a temperature-dependent damage function mapping global mean temperature (and time) to annual monetary losses from premature mortality in the UK. We then combine these two health models using the same meta-analytic framework applied in other sectors: each model is treated as a noisy observation of an underlying, unknown damage function, with model-specific uncertainty carried through from the original studies. The resulting combined health damage function yields central estimates and uncertainty ranges for mortality-related welfare losses as a percentage of UK GDP. The general meta-analysis protocol, including assumptions on error structures and model weighting, is documented in detail in \citet{rising2026comprehensive} and is not repeated here.
