>     \item Fill in unknown values by within a Monte Carlo framework. For each Monte Carlo draw of all channels, we fill in unknown values of channels that have some available values by using a random cross-period scaling from a known CCRA3 channel, and we fill in completely unknown channels by duplicating a randomly selected known CCRA3 channel.
>     \item Construct correlation and double-counting matrices, $C$ and $D$, where correlation is based on being driven by the same hazard and double-counting reflects reported double-counting in \citetalias{watkiss2021monetary}.
>     \item Apply the rapid assessment method described in \citet{rising2022missing} to develop total damage estimates. This consists of constructing functions $u_i = g_i(x_i)$ which translate damages $x_i$ from each channel $i$ to a $\text{Uniform}(0, 1)$ distribution, and modeling the joint distribution of these as a Gaussian Copula with correlation matrix $C$, from which Monte Carl draws $(v_1, v_2, \dots v_n)$ are drawn. The total damages are then $r \cdot (g_1^{-1}(v_1), g_2^{-1}(v_2), \dots, g_n^{-1}(v_n))$ where is the remaining non-double-counted damages after damage channel $i$: $r_1 = 1$ and $r_i = 1 - \sum_{k<i} D_{ik} r_k$.
>     \item Construct a damage function by fitting a quadratic through the available periods (see figure \ref{fig:missrisk-news-damagefunc}).
>     \item Project results by drawing from this damage function for under our normal projection scenarios and periods.
> \end{enumerate}
> 
> \begin{figure}[htbp]
>     \centering
>     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/compare/damagefunc.pdf}
>     \caption{Distributions of missing risks, based on CCRA3 reported values. Grey box plots are used for the 2011--2030 and 2041--2060 periods, and the damage function is used for the 2081--2100 period.}
>     \label{fig:missrisk-news-damagefunc}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \subsubsection{Estimation of Missing Risks from Known Risks}
> 
> To estimate the impacts of missing risks as a fraction of the known risks, following \citet{nordhaus2013climate}, we do the following:
> 
> \begin{enumerate}
> \item We first compute all known risks for each available model as described in the other sections. For droughts, which include a double-counting issues, we adjust the total accordingly
> 
> \item We conduct a Monte Carlo simulation over 1000 iterations. In each iteration, we randomly select a model for each channel by sampling from the available sources for the respective impact channels. 
> 
> \item In each Monte Carlo iteration, we sum the damages for known risks from the selected sources. We then compute the missing risks as 25\% of the total estimated known risks within that Monte Carlo.
> \end{enumerate}
> 
> Through this approach, we derive an estimate of missing risks: 0.66\% GDP [0.19–1.21\%] in the current period, which is predicted to rise to 2.06\% GDP [-0.86 to 5.11\%] by the end of the century.
> ```
